/* Pharmacology 12–13: Anticancer, immunosuppressants, misc; anaesthetics */
Z("pharm12",0,"ph",["Anticancer drugs kill or stop the growth of rapidly dividing cancer cells; they are classified as cytotoxic agents (alkylating agents, antimetabolites, natural products, platinum compounds), hormones and targeted therapies; cisplatin (DNA cross-linker) and methotrexate (folate antagonist) are key examples.","Cancer cells divide fast, so most chemotherapy attacks DNA or cell division — which is why fast-dividing normal cells (marrow, gut lining, hair) are also hit.",["Classification: alkylating agents (cyclophosphamide, ifosfamide, chlorambucil, melphalan, busulfan, nitrosoureas), platinum (cisplatin, carboplatin, oxaliplatin), antimetabolites (methotrexate, 5-FU, capecitabine, 6-MP, cytarabine, gemcitabine), natural products (vincristine, vinblastine, paclitaxel, docetaxel, etoposide, irinotecan, doxorubicin, bleomycin, actinomycin D), hormones (tamoxifen, letrozole, leuprolide, bicalutamide, prednisolone), targeted (imatinib, gefitinib, trastuzumab, rituximab, bevacizumab), immunotherapy (pembrolizumab, nivolumab).","Cisplatin: aquated in cell → intra/interstrand DNA cross-links (N7 guanine) → apoptosis; cell-cycle non-specific; uses — testicular, ovarian, bladder, lung, head & neck, cervical cancers; ADR — nephrotoxicity (hydrate, mannitol, amifostine), severe vomiting (5-HT3 + NK1 + dexamethasone), ototoxicity, neuropathy, hypomagnesaemia, myelosuppression (mild).","Methotrexate: inhibits DHFR → ↓ THF → ↓ thymidylate/purines; S-phase specific; uses — ALL, choriocarcinoma, osteosarcoma (high-dose with leucovorin rescue), breast, head & neck; RA, psoriasis (weekly low dose), ectopic pregnancy, medical abortion; ADR — mucositis, myelosuppression, hepatotoxicity/fibrosis, pneumonitis, nephrotoxicity (high dose), teratogenic; antidote — folinic acid (leucovorin), glucarpidase; NSAIDs, probenecid, cotrimoxazole ↑ toxicity."],"MTX toxicity rescued by Leucovorin; cisplatin = 'kidney, ear, nerve, vomit'."],
"Anticancer (antineoplastic) drugs act by inhibiting cell proliferation, inducing apoptosis or blocking signals driving tumour growth. Classification: (A) cytotoxic drugs — (1) alkylating agents: nitrogen mustards — mechlorethamine, cyclophosphamide, ifosfamide, chlorambucil, melphalan, bendamustine; ethylenimines — thiotepa; alkyl sulfonate — busulfan; nitrosoureas — carmustine, lomustine; triazenes — dacarbazine, temozolomide, procarbazine; (2) platinum coordination complexes: cisplatin, carboplatin, oxaliplatin; (3) antimetabolites: folate antagonists — methotrexate, pemetrexed, pralatrexate; purine antagonists — 6-mercaptopurine, 6-thioguanine, fludarabine, cladribine; pyrimidine antagonists — 5-fluorouracil, capecitabine, cytarabine, gemcitabine, azacitidine; (4) microtubule damaging agents: vinca alkaloids — vincristine, vinblastine, vinorelbine; taxanes — paclitaxel, docetaxel, cabazitaxel; epothilones; eribulin; (5) topoisomerase inhibitors: topoisomerase II — etoposide, teniposide; topoisomerase I — topotecan, irinotecan; (6) antitumour antibiotics: anthracyclines — doxorubicin, daunorubicin, epirubicin, idarubicin; mitoxantrone; bleomycin; actinomycin D (dactinomycin); mitomycin C; (7) miscellaneous: hydroxyurea, L-asparaginase, tretinoin (ATRA), arsenic trioxide, bortezomib; (B) hormonal agents — glucocorticoids (prednisolone — lymphomas, ALL), oestrogens, antioestrogens/SERMs (tamoxifen, toremifene, fulvestrant), aromatase inhibitors (letrozole, anastrozole, exemestane), antiandrogens (flutamide, bicalutamide, enzalutamide), abiraterone, GnRH agonists/antagonists (leuprolide, goserelin, degarelix), progestins (medroxyprogesterone); (C) targeted therapy — tyrosine kinase inhibitors (imatinib — BCR-ABL in CML, GIST; gefitinib, erlotinib, osimertinib — EGFR in lung cancer; sorafenib, sunitinib, lenvatinib), monoclonal antibodies (rituximab — CD20; trastuzumab — HER2; cetuximab — EGFR; bevacizumab — VEGF), mTOR inhibitors, PARP inhibitors (olaparib), CDK4/6 inhibitors (palbociclib), BTK inhibitors (ibrutinib), BCL-2 inhibitor (venetoclax); (D) immunotherapy — immune checkpoint inhibitors (pembrolizumab, nivolumab — PD-1; atezolizumab — PD-L1; ipilimumab — CTLA-4), CAR-T cells, interferon-α, interleukin-2, BCG (bladder). Cell cycle specificity: cell cycle–specific (phase-specific) drugs — S phase: antimetabolites (methotrexate, 5-FU, cytarabine), hydroxyurea; M phase: vinca alkaloids, taxanes; G2: bleomycin, etoposide (late S/G2); cell cycle–non-specific — alkylating agents, platinum compounds, anthracyclines, nitrosoureas (effective on resting and dividing cells). Principles of chemotherapy: log-kill hypothesis, combination chemotherapy (drugs with different mechanisms and non-overlapping toxicities — e.g., ABVD, CHOP, BEP), intermittent pulse dosing (marrow recovery), adjuvant and neoadjuvant therapy, supportive care (antiemetics, growth factors, allopurinol/rasburicase for tumour lysis syndrome, mesna for cyclophosphamide/ifosfamide cystitis, leucovorin, dexrazoxane for anthracycline cardiotoxicity). Common toxicities: bone marrow suppression (neutropenia, thrombocytopenia, anaemia — infection, bleeding), GI mucositis, stomatitis, diarrhoea, nausea and vomiting, alopecia, gonadal toxicity (sterility, amenorrhoea), teratogenicity, carcinogenicity (secondary leukaemia), hyperuricaemia, immunosuppression, extravasation injury, and drug-specific organ toxicities. Cisplatin: mechanism — enters cells passively/by copper transporter; inside the low-chloride intracellular environment the chloride ligands are replaced by water (aquation), forming a highly reactive positively charged species that binds N7 of guanine (and adenine) forming intrastrand (1,2-d(GpG)) and interstrand DNA cross-links and DNA–protein cross-links; distortion of DNA inhibits replication and transcription and triggers apoptosis; cell cycle non-specific; resistance — reduced uptake, glutathione/metallothionein inactivation, enhanced nucleotide excision repair; pharmacokinetics — IV; > 90% protein bound; slowly excreted renally; dose 50–120 mg/m² every 3–4 weeks or weekly 40 mg/m²; uses — testicular germ cell tumours (BEP regimen — curative), ovarian cancer (with paclitaxel; carboplatin preferred), bladder cancer, small cell and non-small cell lung cancer, head and neck cancers (with radiotherapy), carcinoma cervix (chemoradiation), oesophageal, gastric cancer, osteosarcoma, mesothelioma, neuroblastoma; adverse effects — nephrotoxicity (dose-limiting; proximal tubular injury, AKI, hypomagnesaemia, hypokalaemia, hypocalcaemia; prevented by vigorous hydration with saline, mannitol diuresis, magnesium supplements, avoiding other nephrotoxins — aminoglycosides; amifostine), severe nausea and vomiting (highly emetogenic — acute and delayed; prophylaxis with 5-HT3 antagonist + NK1 antagonist + dexamethasone ± olanzapine), ototoxicity (tinnitus, high-frequency hearing loss — especially children; sodium thiosulfate protects), peripheral sensory neuropathy (cumulative, 'stocking-glove'), myelosuppression (mild), anaphylactoid reactions, electrolyte disturbances, Raynaud phenomenon, secondary leukaemia, teratogenicity. Carboplatin — less nephro-, oto- and neurotoxic and less emetogenic, but more myelosuppressive (thrombocytopenia) — dosed by Calvert formula (AUC); oxaliplatin — colorectal cancer (FOLFOX), cold-induced acute neuropathy. Methotrexate: folic acid analogue; mechanism — enters cells via reduced folate carrier, polyglutamated (retained), tightly binds and inhibits dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR), depleting tetrahydrofolate needed for thymidylate synthesis (by thymidylate synthase) and de novo purine synthesis — inhibits DNA synthesis; S-phase specific; at low doses (RA) its anti-inflammatory effect is due to AICAR transformylase inhibition and adenosine release; resistance — reduced transport, decreased polyglutamation, DHFR gene amplification/mutation; pharmacokinetics — oral (low dose), IM, SC, IV, intrathecal; distributes into third-space fluids (ascites, pleural effusion — prolonged toxicity); poor CSF penetration at conventional doses; excreted by kidneys (glomerular filtration and tubular secretion) — toxicity increased in renal impairment; uses — oncology: acute lymphoblastic leukaemia (maintenance, CNS prophylaxis — intrathecal), choriocarcinoma and gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (curative), non-Hodgkin lymphoma, primary CNS lymphoma, osteosarcoma (high-dose with leucovorin rescue), breast cancer (CMF), head and neck cancer, bladder cancer; non-oncological: rheumatoid arthritis (anchor DMARD, 7.5–25 mg once weekly with folic acid), psoriasis and psoriatic arthritis, juvenile idiopathic arthritis, Crohn disease, vasculitis, SLE, sarcoidosis, myasthenia, ectopic pregnancy (single IM dose 50 mg/m²), medical termination of pregnancy (with misoprostol), graft-versus-host disease prophylaxis; adverse effects — myelosuppression (leucopenia, thrombocytopenia, megaloblastic anaemia), mucositis, stomatitis, oral ulcers, diarrhoea, haemorrhagic enteritis, alopecia, hepatotoxicity (raised transaminases, fibrosis and cirrhosis with chronic low-dose use — avoid alcohol; monitor LFTs), pneumonitis (hypersensitivity, interstitial), nephrotoxicity (high-dose — precipitation in acidic urine; prevented by hydration and urine alkalinisation), neurotoxicity (intrathecal — arachnoiditis, leukoencephalopathy), teratogenicity and abortifacient (contraception mandatory; stop 3 months before conception), skin rashes, photosensitivity, osteoporosis, infections; dosing errors (daily instead of weekly) cause fatal toxicity; antidote — folinic acid (leucovorin; bypasses DHFR block; 'leucovorin rescue' started 24 h after high-dose MTX and continued until levels fall), glucarpidase (carboxypeptidase G2) for toxic levels with renal failure; interactions — NSAIDs, aspirin, probenecid, penicillins, PPIs (reduce renal clearance — toxicity), cotrimoxazole and trimethoprim (additive antifolate — pancytopenia), alcohol (hepatotoxicity), live vaccines.",
[["Classification",[["Group","Drugs"],["Alkylating agents","Cyclophosphamide, ifosfamide, chlorambucil, melphalan, busulfan, carmustine, temozolomide"],["Platinum compounds","Cisplatin, carboplatin, oxaliplatin"],["Antimetabolites","Methotrexate, 5-FU, capecitabine, 6-MP, cytarabine, gemcitabine"],["Microtubule agents","Vincristine, vinblastine, paclitaxel, docetaxel"],["Topoisomerase inhibitors","Etoposide, irinotecan, topotecan"],["Antitumour antibiotics","Doxorubicin, daunorubicin, bleomycin, actinomycin D, mitomycin"],["Hormonal","Tamoxifen, letrozole, bicalutamide, leuprolide, prednisolone"],["Targeted","Imatinib, gefitinib, trastuzumab, rituximab, bevacizumab"],["Immunotherapy","Pembrolizumab, nivolumab, ipilimumab, CAR-T"]]],
["Cisplatin vs methotrexate",[["Feature","Cisplatin","Methotrexate"],["Class","Platinum (alkylating-like)","Antimetabolite (folate antagonist)"],["MOA","DNA cross-links","DHFR inhibition"],["Cell cycle","Non-specific","S-phase specific"],["Uses","Testicular, ovarian, bladder, lung, head & neck, cervix","ALL, choriocarcinoma, osteosarcoma; RA, psoriasis, ectopic pregnancy"],["Dose-limiting ADR","Nephrotoxicity","Myelosuppression, mucositis"],["Other ADR","Vomiting, ototoxicity, neuropathy, ↓ Mg²⁺","Hepatotoxicity, pneumonitis, teratogenic"],["Protection/antidote","Hydration, mannitol, Mg²⁺, amifostine","Leucovorin, glucarpidase"]]],
["Specific toxicities and protectants",[["Drug","Toxicity","Protectant"],["Cyclophosphamide/ifosfamide","Haemorrhagic cystitis","Mesna"],["Doxorubicin","Cardiotoxicity","Dexrazoxane"],["Bleomycin","Pulmonary fibrosis","Limit cumulative dose"],["Vincristine","Neuropathy","Dose cap"],["Cisplatin","Nephro/ototoxicity","Hydration, amifostine, thiosulfate"],["Methotrexate","Marrow, mucositis","Leucovorin"],["Tumour lysis","Hyperuricaemia","Allopurinol, rasburicase"]]]],
{i:"Anticancer drugs are used in combinations exploiting different mechanisms and non-overlapping toxicities, together with supportive care.",f:"Cell cycle: G1 → S (antimetabolites: MTX, 5-FU) → G2 (bleomycin, etoposide) → M (vincristine, taxanes)\n Cell-cycle non-specific: alkylating agents, cisplatin, anthracyclines\n MTX → DHFR ✕ → ↓ THF → ↓ dTMP/purines; leucovorin bypass",c:"Cisplatin and methotrexate illustrate DNA-damaging and antimetabolite chemotherapy; each has characteristic toxicities that are prevented or rescued by specific measures such as hydration and leucovorin."});

Z("pharm12",1,"ph",["Immunosuppressants reduce immune responses to prevent organ transplant rejection and treat autoimmune diseases; cyclosporine is a calcineurin inhibitor that blocks T-cell activation by preventing IL-2 production.","Cyclosporine cuts the 'alarm wire' (calcineurin–NFAT) that tells T cells to make IL-2, so the T-cell army never mobilises.",["Classification: calcineurin inhibitors (cyclosporine, tacrolimus), mTOR inhibitors (sirolimus, everolimus), antiproliferatives (azathioprine, mycophenolate, methotrexate, cyclophosphamide, leflunomide), glucocorticoids (prednisolone, methylprednisolone), biologics (ATG, basiliximab — IL-2R, belatacept, alemtuzumab, rituximab, anti-TNF), JAK inhibitors.","Cyclosporine MOA: binds cyclophilin → complex inhibits calcineurin → NFAT not dephosphorylated → ↓ IL-2 transcription → ↓ T-cell proliferation; no marrow suppression.","Uses: organ transplantation (kidney, liver, heart), GVHD, psoriasis, RA, nephrotic syndrome (steroid-dependent), aplastic anaemia, atopic dermatitis, dry eye (topical), uveitis.","ADR: nephrotoxicity (dose-limiting), hypertension, hyperkalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, hepatotoxicity, neurotoxicity (tremor, seizures, PRES), hirsutism, gum hyperplasia, hyperuricaemia, hyperlipidaemia, diabetes, infections, lymphoma/skin cancer; CYP3A4 substrate — TDM; ↑ by ketoconazole, erythromycin, diltiazem, grapefruit; ↓ by rifampicin, phenytoin."],"Cyclosporine ADR: 'nephrotoxic, hypertension, hirsutism, gum hyperplasia'."],
"Immunosuppressants inhibit the immune response; they are used in organ and bone marrow transplantation (induction, maintenance, treatment of rejection) and autoimmune/inflammatory diseases. Classification: (1) calcineurin inhibitors — cyclosporine (ciclosporin), tacrolimus; (2) mTOR inhibitors (proliferation signal inhibitors) — sirolimus (rapamycin), everolimus; (3) antiproliferative/cytotoxic agents — azathioprine, mycophenolate mofetil/sodium (inhibits IMP dehydrogenase — lymphocyte purine synthesis), methotrexate, cyclophosphamide, leflunomide, chlorambucil; (4) glucocorticoids — prednisolone, methylprednisolone, dexamethasone; (5) biological agents — polyclonal antibodies (antithymocyte globulin), monoclonal antibodies (basiliximab — anti-IL-2 receptor CD25; alemtuzumab — CD52; rituximab — CD20; muromonab-CD3 — obsolete), fusion proteins (belatacept — CTLA-4–Ig blocks CD80/86 costimulation; abatacept), cytokine inhibitors (anti-TNF: infliximab, adalimumab, etanercept; anti-IL-6: tocilizumab; anti-IL-17, IL-23), complement inhibitor (eculizumab); (6) JAK inhibitors — tofacitinib, baricitinib; (7) S1P receptor modulators — fingolimod. Cyclosporine: cyclic polypeptide of 11 amino acids from the fungus Tolypocladium inflatum. Mechanism: lipophilic; enters T lymphocytes and binds the cytoplasmic immunophilin cyclophilin; the cyclosporine–cyclophilin complex binds and inhibits calcineurin, a Ca²⁺/calmodulin-dependent serine phosphatase; normally after T-cell receptor activation calcineurin dephosphorylates the nuclear factor of activated T cells (NFAT), allowing it to translocate to the nucleus and induce transcription of IL-2 and other cytokines (IL-3, IL-4, IFN-γ, TNF-α, GM-CSF); inhibition prevents IL-2 production and IL-2 receptor expression, blocking clonal proliferation of helper and cytotoxic T cells (G0 → G1); selective for T-cell–mediated immunity, spares phagocytic function and does not cause bone marrow suppression. Pharmacokinetics: variable and incomplete oral absorption (microemulsion formulation — Neoral — more consistent), IV available; highly lipophilic, extensive tissue distribution, bound to lipoproteins; metabolised by CYP3A4 and P-gp in gut and liver; biliary excretion; t½ ~ 8–24 h; therapeutic drug monitoring (trough C0 or 2-hour C2 levels) essential due to narrow therapeutic index and interactions. Uses: (1) organ transplantation — kidney, liver, heart, lung, pancreas (prevention of rejection, usually with mycophenolate and steroids; tacrolimus now preferred); (2) bone marrow/haematopoietic stem cell transplantation — prevention and treatment of graft-versus-host disease (with methotrexate); (3) autoimmune and inflammatory diseases — severe psoriasis, atopic dermatitis, rheumatoid arthritis, steroid-dependent/resistant nephrotic syndrome (minimal change, FSGS), aplastic anaemia (with ATG), ulcerative colitis (acute severe, steroid-refractory — IV rescue), uveitis, Behçet disease, myasthenia gravis, chronic urticaria; (4) topical — 0.05% eye drops for dry eye disease (keratoconjunctivitis sicca), vernal keratoconjunctivitis. Adverse effects: nephrotoxicity (most important, dose-limiting; acute — afferent arteriolar vasoconstriction, reduced GFR, reversible; chronic — interstitial fibrosis, tubular atrophy, arteriolopathy; monitor creatinine), hypertension (vasoconstriction, Na⁺ retention — treat with CCB such as amlodipine), hyperkalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, hyperuricaemia and gout, hepatotoxicity, neurotoxicity (tremor, paraesthesia, headache, seizures, posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome), hirsutism and hypertrichosis, gingival hyperplasia, hyperlipidaemia, glucose intolerance/new-onset diabetes after transplant (less than tacrolimus), thrombotic microangiopathy, increased susceptibility to infections (viral — CMV, BK virus, EBV; fungal; Pneumocystis), increased risk of malignancy (skin cancers, post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder — EBV), anaphylaxis with IV preparation (Cremophor vehicle), nausea, gum and hair changes. Drug interactions (CYP3A4/P-gp): increased levels and toxicity with ketoconazole, itraconazole, fluconazole, voriconazole, erythromycin, clarithromycin, diltiazem, verapamil, nicardipine, amiodarone, HIV protease inhibitors, grapefruit juice, allopurinol, metoclopramide; decreased levels and rejection risk with rifampicin, phenytoin, carbamazepine, phenobarbitone, St John's wort; additive nephrotoxicity with aminoglycosides, amphotericin B, NSAIDs, vancomycin; hyperkalaemia with K⁺-sparing diuretics, ACEI; cyclosporine raises levels of statins (myopathy), digoxin, colchicine. Tacrolimus (binds FKBP-12; 10–100 times more potent; less hirsutism and gum hyperplasia, more neurotoxicity and diabetes) is now the preferred calcineurin inhibitor in transplantation.",
[["Classification",[["Group","Drugs","Target"],["Calcineurin inhibitors","Cyclosporine, tacrolimus","Calcineurin → IL-2"],["mTOR inhibitors","Sirolimus, everolimus","mTOR → IL-2 response"],["Antiproliferatives","Azathioprine, mycophenolate, methotrexate, cyclophosphamide","DNA/purine synthesis"],["Glucocorticoids","Prednisolone, methylprednisolone","Multiple cytokines"],["Biologics","ATG, basiliximab, belatacept, rituximab, anti-TNF","Cells/cytokines"],["JAK inhibitors","Tofacitinib, baricitinib","JAK–STAT"]]],
["Cyclosporine vs tacrolimus",[["Feature","Cyclosporine","Tacrolimus"],["Immunophilin","Cyclophilin","FKBP-12"],["Potency","Lower","10–100× higher"],["Nephrotoxicity","Yes","Yes"],["Hirsutism, gum hyperplasia","Yes","No"],["Neurotoxicity, diabetes","Less","More"],["Hypertension, hyperlipidaemia","More","Less"]]],
["Adverse effects of cyclosporine",["Nephrotoxicity","Hypertension","Hyperkalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, hyperuricaemia","Hirsutism, gingival hyperplasia","Tremor, seizures, PRES","Hepatotoxicity, hyperlipidaemia, diabetes","Infections, lymphoma, skin cancer"]]],
{i:"Calcineurin inhibitors revolutionised transplantation by enabling selective T-cell suppression without marrow toxicity.",f:"Antigen + TCR → ↑ Ca²⁺ → calmodulin → calcineurin\n → dephosphorylates NFAT → nucleus → IL-2 gene → T-cell proliferation\n Cyclosporine + cyclophilin ⊣ calcineurin (tacrolimus + FKBP)\n Sirolimus ⊣ mTOR (IL-2 receptor signalling)",c:"Cyclosporine selectively blocks T-cell activation via calcineurin inhibition; its narrow therapeutic index, nephrotoxicity and many CYP3A4 interactions demand drug-level monitoring."});

Z("pharm12",2,"ph",["Chelating agents bind toxic heavy metals into stable, water-soluble complexes that are excreted; key examples are BAL (dimercaprol) for arsenic, mercury and lead, CaNa2EDTA for lead, penicillamine for copper (Wilson's disease), and desferrioxamine for iron.","Chelators are molecular 'claws' that grab metal ions tightly and carry them out in urine or bile.",["BAL (dimercaprol): SH groups bind As, Hg, Pb, Au; IM in oil (painful); uses — arsenic, mercury (inorganic), gold, lead encephalopathy (with EDTA); CI — iron, cadmium (toxic complexes), G6PD; ADR — hypertension, tachycardia, burning, lacrimation.","CaNa2EDTA: exchanges Ca for lead → excreted; IV/IM; lead poisoning (with BAL in encephalopathy); ADR — nephrotoxicity; never use Na2EDTA (hypocalcaemia/tetany). Succimer (DMSA) oral for lead in children.","Penicillamine: oral; copper (Wilson's disease), cystinuria, lead, RA (historical); ADR — rash, proteinuria/nephrotic, bone marrow suppression, loss of taste, lupus, myasthenia, pyridoxine deficiency.","Desferrioxamine: binds Fe³⁺ (ferrioxamine, vin rosé urine); acute iron poisoning (IV), transfusion haemosiderosis (SC infusion), aluminium toxicity; ADR — hypotension, ototoxicity, retinopathy, Yersinia; oral alternatives — deferiprone, deferasirox."],"Metal → chelator: Iron–DFO, Copper–penicillamine, Lead–EDTA/BAL/succimer, Arsenic/Mercury–BAL."],
"Chelating agents are compounds that form stable, non-toxic, water-soluble ring complexes (chelates) with metal ions by coordinate bonds via two or more electron-donor groups (–SH, –OH, –COOH, –NH2), thus preventing or reversing binding of metals to tissue ligands (enzymes, proteins) and promoting their excretion. Ideal properties: high affinity for the toxic metal, low affinity for essential Ca²⁺ and Zn²⁺, water solubility, resistance to metabolism, ability to reach sites of metal storage, low toxicity of the chelate, and good excretion. Dimercaprol (British Anti-Lewisite, BAL): developed as antidote to the arsenical war gas lewisite; oily liquid with two SH groups that compete with tissue sulfhydryl groups for metals forming stable chelates; given by deep IM injection in arachis (peanut) oil (3–5 mg/kg every 4–6 h); short t½, excreted in urine and bile; uses — arsenic poisoning (acute and chronic), inorganic mercury poisoning (acute), gold toxicity, lead poisoning (with CaNa2EDTA in lead encephalopathy or blood lead > 70 µg/dL — BAL given first to prevent redistribution of lead to brain), antimony, bismuth, copper (adjunct in Wilson disease); contraindicated in iron, cadmium, selenium poisoning (BAL–metal complex more toxic, nephrotoxic), organic mercury (methylmercury — increases brain levels), peanut allergy, G6PD deficiency (haemolysis), hepatic insufficiency; adverse effects (dose-related, common) — rise in BP, tachycardia, nausea, vomiting, headache, burning sensation of lips, mouth, throat and eyes, lacrimation, salivation, rhinorrhoea, sweating, muscle aches, tingling, anxiety, pain and sterile abscess at injection site, fever in children; alkalinise urine to prevent dissociation of chelate in kidney. Calcium disodium edetate (CaNa2EDTA): calcium chelate of EDTA; exchanges calcium for metals with higher affinity — lead (most important), zinc, cadmium, manganese, iron, copper; not absorbed orally — IV infusion (preferred) or IM (with procaine); distributed extracellularly (does not enter cells or CSF well — hence combination with BAL in encephalopathy); excreted unchanged by glomerular filtration (lead chelate); uses — lead poisoning (symptomatic, encephalopathy with BAL, blood lead > 45–70 µg/dL), lead mobilisation test; adverse effects — nephrotoxicity (proximal tubular necrosis — hydrate, monitor urine), fever, malaise, myalgia, histamine-like reactions, zinc depletion, thrombophlebitis, transient hypotension, increased ICP in encephalopathy (infuse slowly); disodium EDTA (Na2EDTA) must not be used for lead poisoning (chelates serum calcium — fatal hypocalcaemic tetany; formerly used for hypercalcaemia). Succimer (meso-2,3-dimercaptosuccinic acid, DMSA): water-soluble oral BAL analogue; preferred for lead poisoning in children with blood lead 45–69 µg/dL without encephalopathy, also arsenic and mercury; GI upset, rash, raised transaminases; less zinc and copper loss. Unithiol (DMPS): mercury and arsenic. Penicillamine (D-β,β-dimethylcysteine): degradation product of penicillin; oral; chelates copper, mercury, zinc, lead; forms disulfide with cystine (soluble — prevents cystine stones); immunomodulatory (inhibits T cells, macrophages, reduces rheumatoid factor) and anti-fibrotic (inhibits collagen cross-linking); uses — Wilson disease (hepatolenticular degeneration; promotes urinary copper excretion; lifelong with pyridoxine; trientine and zinc acetate are alternatives — zinc blocks intestinal copper absorption), cystinuria (prevents/dissolves cystine stones), lead and mercury poisoning (alternative), rheumatoid arthritis (historical second-line DMARD), scleroderma (historical), primary biliary cholangitis (obsolete); adverse effects — frequent and sometimes serious: rashes, pruritus, urticaria, pemphigus, loss of taste (zinc chelation), nausea, proteinuria and nephrotic syndrome (membranous nephropathy), haematuria, bone marrow suppression (leucopenia, thrombocytopenia, aplastic anaemia), drug-induced lupus, myasthenia gravis, Goodpasture syndrome, polymyositis, pyridoxine deficiency (give 25 mg/day), cutis laxa (elastosis perforans), worsening of neurological symptoms early in Wilson disease; cross-allergy with penicillin (rare); contraindicated in pregnancy (except Wilson), renal disease. Desferrioxamine (deferoxamine): obtained from Streptomyces pilosus; hexadentate chelator with very high affinity for ferric iron (1 molecule binds 1 Fe³⁺; 100 mg binds 8.5 mg iron) and aluminium; very low affinity for calcium; removes iron from ferritin and haemosiderin (not from haemoglobin, transferrin or cytochromes); poorly absorbed orally — given IV, SC or IM; short t½ (1 h); ferrioxamine excreted in urine (reddish 'vin rosé' colour) and bile; uses — acute iron poisoning (IV infusion 15 mg/kg/h; maximum 80 mg/kg/24 h), chronic iron overload/transfusion haemosiderosis (thalassaemia major, sickle cell, myelodysplasia, aplastic anaemia) — slow SC infusion 20–60 mg/kg over 8–12 hours 5–7 nights/week via portable pump, with vitamin C (enhances excretion), haemochromatosis (when phlebotomy contraindicated), aluminium toxicity in dialysis patients (dialysis encephalopathy, osteodystrophy), diagnosis of iron overload (desferrioxamine test); adverse effects — rapid IV infusion: flushing, urticaria, hypotension, shock; local reactions (pain, induration with SC), ARDS (prolonged high-dose IV), ototoxicity (high-frequency hearing loss), retinal toxicity (night blindness, visual field defects), growth retardation and skeletal changes in children, increased susceptibility to Yersinia enterocolitica and mucormycosis (siderophore used by organisms), renal impairment. Oral iron chelators: deferiprone (bidentate; agranulocytosis — weekly counts; arthropathy), deferasirox (tridentate, once daily; renal and hepatic toxicity, GI bleeding).",
[["Chelating agents",[["Agent","Route","Metals / uses","Key ADR"],["BAL (dimercaprol)","IM (oil)","Arsenic, inorganic mercury, gold, lead (with EDTA)","Hypertension, tachycardia, burning, lacrimation; CI in Fe, Cd"],["CaNa2EDTA","IV/IM","Lead","Nephrotoxicity"],["Succimer (DMSA)","Oral","Lead (children), As, Hg","GI, rash"],["Penicillamine","Oral","Copper (Wilson), cystinuria, lead, RA","Rash, proteinuria, marrow suppression, lupus, myasthenia"],["Desferrioxamine","IV/SC","Iron (acute, chronic overload), aluminium","Hypotension, oto/retinal toxicity, Yersinia"],["Deferiprone / deferasirox","Oral","Chronic iron overload","Agranulocytosis / renal toxicity"],["Trientine, zinc","Oral","Wilson disease","—"]]],
["Metal → antidote",[["Metal","Antidote"],["Lead","CaNa2EDTA, BAL (encephalopathy), succimer"],["Arsenic","BAL, DMSA, DMPS"],["Mercury (inorganic)","BAL, DMPS, succimer"],["Copper (Wilson)","Penicillamine, trientine, zinc"],["Iron","Desferrioxamine"],["Gold","BAL"],["Cadmium","CaNa2EDTA (not BAL)"]]]],
{c:"Each heavy metal has preferred chelators — BAL for arsenic and mercury, EDTA and succimer for lead, penicillamine for copper and desferrioxamine for iron — chosen for affinity, route and toxicity, avoiding combinations that form toxic complexes."});

Z("pharm12",3,"ph",["Vitamin A (retinol) is a fat-soluble vitamin essential for vision, epithelial integrity, immunity and growth; vitamin C (ascorbic acid) is a water-soluble antioxidant needed for collagen synthesis and iron absorption.","Vitamin A is the 'eye and skin guardian' — it makes the visual pigment rhodopsin and keeps linings moist; vitamin C is the 'collagen builder' — without it, gums bleed and wounds don't heal (scurvy).",["Vitamin A: retinal → rhodopsin (dim-light vision); retinoic acid → nuclear RAR/RXR → epithelial differentiation; deficiency — night blindness, xerophthalmia (Bitot's spots, keratomalacia), follicular hyperkeratosis, infections.","Vitamin A uses: prophylaxis (National programme: 1 lakh IU at 9 months, then 2 lakh IU every 6 months to 5 years), xerophthalmia treatment (2 lakh IU days 0, 1, 14), measles (reduces mortality), retinoids — tretinoin (acne, APL), isotretinoin (severe acne — teratogenic), acitretin (psoriasis).","Vitamin C: cofactor for prolyl/lysyl hydroxylase (collagen), dopamine β-hydroxylase, carnitine synthesis; reduces Fe³⁺ to Fe²⁺ (↑ absorption); antioxidant; deficiency — scurvy (bleeding gums, perifollicular haemorrhages, poor wound healing, subperiosteal haemorrhage, anaemia).","Vitamin C uses: scurvy, with iron for anaemia, methaemoglobinaemia (alternative), urine acidification, with desferrioxamine; ADR (megadoses) — oxalate stones, diarrhoea, haemolysis (G6PD), iron overload; hypervitaminosis A — raised ICP, hepatotoxicity, teratogenic (avoid > 10,000 IU/day in pregnancy)."],"Vitamin A deficiency: night blindness first; vitamin C deficiency: scurvy."],
"Vitamin A comprises retinoids — retinol (alcohol), retinal (aldehyde) and retinoic acid — and provitamin A carotenoids (β-carotene from yellow-orange fruits, green leafy vegetables, converted in intestine); preformed vitamin A is found in liver, fish liver oils, egg yolk, milk, butter. Daily requirement: about 600 µg retinol activity equivalents (2000–3000 IU) in adults; more in pregnancy and lactation. Absorption requires bile and fat; stored in liver (Ito/stellate cells); transported by retinol-binding protein. Physiological functions: (1) vision — 11-cis-retinal combines with opsin to form rhodopsin in rods; light isomerises it to all-trans-retinal triggering visual signal; deficiency impairs dark adaptation (night blindness); (2) epithelial integrity and differentiation — retinoic acid binds nuclear retinoic acid receptors (RAR) and retinoid X receptors (RXR), regulating genes for epithelial differentiation and mucus secretion; deficiency causes keratinising metaplasia (xerosis of conjunctiva and cornea, follicular hyperkeratosis/phrynoderma, respiratory and urinary epithelial changes); (3) immunity — supports T- and B-cell function and mucosal barriers; deficiency increases severity and mortality of measles, diarrhoea, respiratory infections; (4) growth, bone development, reproduction (spermatogenesis, placental development), embryonic development (morphogen). Deficiency: night blindness (earliest), conjunctival xerosis, Bitot's spots (foamy triangular patches), corneal xerosis, ulceration and keratomalacia (blindness — WHO classification X1–X3), xerophthalmic fundus, follicular hyperkeratosis, growth retardation, anaemia, increased infections; leading cause of preventable childhood blindness. Preparations and uses: (1) prophylaxis — National Vitamin A Prophylaxis Programme: 1 lakh IU orally at 9 months (with measles–rubella vaccine), then 2 lakh IU every 6 months from 16–18 months up to 5 years (total 9 doses); (2) treatment of xerophthalmia — 2 lakh IU (1 lakh in 6–12 months, 50,000 in < 6 months) on day 0, day 1 and day 14 (WHO schedule); (3) measles — two doses of vitamin A on consecutive days reduce mortality and complications; (4) malabsorption, cholestasis, cystic fibrosis; (5) pharmacological retinoids — tretinoin (all-trans-retinoic acid) topical for acne, photoaging, and oral for acute promyelocytic leukaemia (induces differentiation; 'differentiation syndrome'); isotretinoin (13-cis-retinoic acid) oral for severe nodulocystic acne (highly teratogenic — iPLEDGE, contraception; dry skin, cheilitis, hyperlipidaemia, depression); acitretin for psoriasis; bexarotene for cutaneous T-cell lymphoma; adapalene, tazarotene topical. Hypervitaminosis A: acute (very large dose, polar bear liver) — raised intracranial pressure (headache, vomiting, bulging fontanelle in infants), drowsiness, irritability, desquamation; chronic — anorexia, alopecia, dry skin, bone pain and hyperostosis, hepatomegaly and liver fibrosis, pseudotumour cerebri, hypercalcaemia; teratogenic in pregnancy (craniofacial, cardiac, CNS defects) — avoid > 10,000 IU/day supplements and retinoid drugs; carotenaemia (yellow skin, harmless) from excess β-carotene. Vitamin C (ascorbic acid): water-soluble, sugar acid; humans cannot synthesise (lack L-gulonolactone oxidase); sources — citrus fruits, amla (Indian gooseberry — richest), guava, tomatoes, green leafy vegetables, potatoes; destroyed by heat, storage, alkali; requirement 40–80 mg/day (more in smokers, pregnancy, lactation). Functions: powerful reducing agent and antioxidant (reversible oxidation to dehydroascorbic acid); cofactor for hydroxylases — prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases (hydroxylation of proline and lysine in procollagen — stable triple helix; deficiency produces weak collagen), dopamine β-hydroxylase (noradrenaline synthesis), peptidyl α-amidating monooxygenase, enzymes of carnitine synthesis, tyrosine metabolism; promotes absorption of non-haem iron (reduces Fe³⁺ to Fe²⁺, forms soluble chelate) and mobilisation from ferritin; folate metabolism (keeps tetrahydrofolate reduced); steroidogenesis in adrenal; immune function (neutrophil function); regenerates vitamin E. Deficiency — scurvy: fatigue, irritability, gum swelling, sponginess and bleeding (in dentate persons), loosening of teeth, perifollicular haemorrhages and corkscrew hairs, petechiae, ecchymoses, poor wound healing and reopening of old scars, anaemia (iron and folate), haemarthrosis, bone pain; infantile scurvy (Barlow disease) — subperiosteal haemorrhages (painful 'frog-leg' posture, pseudoparalysis), scorbutic rosary, X-ray signs (Wimberger ring, Frankel line, Trümmerfeld zone). Uses: (1) prevention and treatment of scurvy (100–300 mg/day, up to 1 g/day initially); (2) with oral iron to enhance absorption in iron deficiency anaemia; (3) with desferrioxamine to increase iron excretion in iron overload; (4) acquired methaemoglobinaemia (alternative to methylene blue, especially G6PD deficiency); (5) urinary acidification (enhance excretion of basic drugs, with methenamine); (6) wound healing, burns, postoperative states, smokers (supplementation); (7) tyrosinaemia in premature infants; (8) high-dose IV in sepsis (no proven benefit); common cold (no prevention in general population; slight reduction in duration). Adverse effects (megadoses > 1–2 g/day): GI upset, osmotic diarrhoea, heartburn, oxalate and urate kidney stones (avoid in history of stones, renal failure), haemolysis in G6PD deficiency (large IV doses), increased iron absorption and overload in haemochromatosis and thalassaemia, rebound scurvy after sudden withdrawal (conditioned), false-negative occult blood and false glucose results in urine tests; interference with warfarin action.",
[["Vitamin A vs vitamin C",[["Feature","Vitamin A","Vitamin C"],["Solubility","Fat","Water"],["Main functions","Vision (rhodopsin), epithelial differentiation, immunity","Collagen synthesis, antioxidant, iron absorption"],["Deficiency","Night blindness, xerophthalmia, keratomalacia","Scurvy"],["Sources","Liver, fish oil, carrots (β-carotene), leafy greens","Amla, citrus, guava, tomatoes"],["Toxicity","Raised ICP, hepatotoxicity, teratogenic","Oxalate stones, diarrhoea, haemolysis (G6PD)"],["Programme","Vitamin A prophylaxis (9 months–5 years)","—"]]],
["Vitamin A prophylaxis and treatment",[["Group","Dose"],["9 months (with MR vaccine)","1 lakh IU"],["16 months–5 years","2 lakh IU every 6 months"],["Xerophthalmia (> 1 year)","2 lakh IU on day 0, 1, 14"],["6–12 months","1 lakh IU"],["< 6 months","50,000 IU"]]],
["Uses of vitamin C",["Scurvy","With oral iron (absorption)","With desferrioxamine","Methaemoglobinaemia (G6PD)","Urinary acidification","Wound healing, burns"]]],
{c:"Vitamin A is vital for vision, epithelia and immunity and is supplemented nationally to prevent childhood blindness and mortality, while vitamin C supports collagen synthesis and iron absorption; both carry risks at megadoses."});

Z("pharm12",4,"ph",["BCG (Bacillus Calmette–Guérin) is a live attenuated vaccine from Mycobacterium bovis given intradermally at birth to protect infants against severe forms of tuberculosis such as TB meningitis and miliary TB.","BCG is a weakened cousin of the TB germ that trains the baby's immune system early, protecting mainly against the most dangerous childhood forms of TB.",["Vaccine: live attenuated M. bovis (Danish 1331 strain in India); freeze-dried, reconstituted with normal saline; protect from light; use within 4 h.","Schedule: at birth (or as early as possible up to 1 year); dose 0.05 mL (< 1 month) / 0.1 mL intradermal, left upper arm (deltoid insertion); tuberculin-syringe, 26G needle.","Normal reaction: papule (2–3 weeks) → pustule → ulcer → scar (6–12 weeks); ~80% efficacy against TB meningitis/miliary TB, variable (0–80%) against adult pulmonary TB.","Complications: local ulcer/abscess, regional lymphadenitis (suppurative — needle aspiration, isoniazid), keloid, disseminated BCG (immunodeficient — CI in symptomatic HIV, SCID). Other uses: intravesical BCG for superficial bladder cancer (immunotherapy), leprosy protection."],"BCG: 'birth, intradermal, left arm, 0.05 mL, scar = success'."],
"BCG vaccine contains live attenuated bacilli of Mycobacterium bovis, derived by Calmette and Guérin through 230 serial subcultures on glycerine–bile–potato medium (1908–1921). Strains: Danish 1331 (used in India, produced at BCG Vaccine Laboratory, Guindy), Pasteur, Tokyo 172, Moscow. Form: freeze-dried (lyophilised) vaccine in amber ampoules/vials (light-sensitive), reconstituted with normal saline (not distilled water); stored at 2–8 °C (can be frozen in dried form); reconstituted vaccine must be used within 4 hours (discarded at the end of session) because of risk of contamination and loss of potency. Mechanism: induces cell-mediated immunity (Th1, macrophage activation) against mycobacteria; does not prevent primary infection but limits haematogenous dissemination, protecting against severe forms in children. Schedule (Universal Immunisation Programme, India): a single dose at birth or as early as possible until 1 year of age; dose 0.05 mL for infants < 1 month and 0.1 mL for older infants; route — strictly intradermal using a tuberculin syringe with 26-gauge needle, raising a wheal of 5 mm, over the left upper arm at the insertion of deltoid; no prior tuberculin test needed in infants; skin should not be cleaned with spirit (kills live bacilli). Normal course of reaction: a small papule appears in 2–3 weeks, increases to 4–8 mm by 5 weeks, becomes a pustule which bursts forming a shallow ulcer, which heals in 6–12 weeks leaving a permanent, tiny, round depressed scar (4–8 mm); no dressing or antiseptic needed; absence of scar does not necessarily mean lack of protection (revaccination not recommended). Efficacy: high (~80%) against tuberculous meningitis and miliary/disseminated TB in children; highly variable (0–80%) against adult pulmonary TB in different trials (lower in tropical countries — Chingleput trial in India showed no protection, attributed to environmental mycobacteria and genetic factors); protection wanes after 10–15 years; also offers partial protection against leprosy and non-specific beneficial effects on infant mortality (trained immunity). Contraindications: symptomatic HIV infection/AIDS (risk of disseminated BCG-osis; asymptomatic HIV-exposed infants in high-prevalence settings may be vaccinated per national policy), congenital immunodeficiency (SCID, chronic granulomatous disease), immunosuppressive therapy (steroids, chemotherapy), malignancy, pregnancy; generalised eczema/infected skin at site; severe malnutrition is not a contraindication. Complications (rare, more with faulty technique — subcutaneous injection, excessive dose, virulent strain): prolonged severe ulceration, local abscess (subcutaneous injection), suppurative axillary or cervical lymphadenitis (needle aspiration, isoniazid ± rifampicin; surgical excision rarely), keloid formation, lupoid reactions, osteitis, disseminated BCG infection (in immunodeficient — fatal; treat with antitubercular drugs excluding pyrazinamide, as M. bovis is resistant), BCG-IRIS in HIV, accidental eye inoculation. Other uses of BCG: intravesical BCG instillation — immunotherapy for non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer (carcinoma in situ, high-grade Ta/T1) after TURBT — induces local inflammation and immune destruction of tumour cells; cystitis, haematuria, fever, rarely disseminated BCG infection; investigational in type 1 diabetes and as trained immunity booster. Tuberculin (Mantoux) conversion after BCG is variable and does not correlate with protection; BCG may cause false-positive tuberculin skin test (IGRA unaffected).",
[["BCG at a glance",[["Feature","Detail"],["Type","Live attenuated M. bovis (Danish 1331)"],["Schedule","At birth (up to 1 year)"],["Dose","0.05 mL (< 1 month), 0.1 mL (older)"],["Route / site","Intradermal, left upper arm (deltoid insertion)"],["Diluent","Normal saline; use within 4 h"],["Storage","2–8 °C, protect from light"],["Reaction","Papule → pustule → ulcer → scar (6–12 weeks)"],["Efficacy","~80% vs TB meningitis/miliary TB; variable vs pulmonary TB"]]],
["Contraindications",["Symptomatic HIV/AIDS","Congenital immunodeficiency (SCID, CGD)","Immunosuppressive therapy, malignancy","Pregnancy","Generalised skin disease at site"]],
["Complications",["Persistent ulcer, abscess","Suppurative lymphadenitis","Keloid","Osteitis","Disseminated BCG (immunodeficient)"]]],
{c:"BCG, a live attenuated intradermal vaccine given at birth, reliably protects infants against severe disseminated TB though its effect on adult pulmonary TB is variable; it is avoided in immunodeficiency and has a separate role as bladder cancer immunotherapy."});

Z("pharm13",0,"ph",["Guedel's stages describe the progressive depth of general anaesthesia with ether: stage I analgesia, stage II delirium/excitement, stage III surgical anaesthesia (four planes) and stage IV medullary paralysis.","Anaesthetics switch off the brain from top to bottom — first the cortex (pain perception), then the higher centres (excitement), then the spinal cord (reflexes), and finally the medulla (breathing, circulation) — which must never be reached.",["Stage I (analgesia): from induction to loss of consciousness; pain perception reduced; conscious; minor procedures possible.","Stage II (delirium/excitement): from loss of consciousness to regular respiration; irregular breathing, struggling, vomiting, laryngospasm, dilated pupils — dangerous; modern rapid IV induction passes quickly.","Stage III (surgical anaesthesia): from regular respiration to cessation of spontaneous breathing; 4 planes — progressive loss of eyelid/corneal reflexes, eyeballs centralise, pupils dilate, intercostal paralysis (plane 4: diaphragmatic breathing only).","Stage IV (medullary paralysis): respiratory and vasomotor centre failure → death unless reversed; modern monitoring (BIS, end-tidal agent) replaces signs."],"Guedel stages: 'Analgesia, Delirium, Surgical, Medullary'."],
"Guedel (1937) described four stages of general anaesthesia based on the slow induction with diethyl ether in premedicated (with morphine and atropine) patients; the stages reflect a descending order of depression of CNS — cortical centres, subcortical centres, spinal cord and finally medullary centres (respiratory and vasomotor) which are least sensitive. Stage I — stage of analgesia: from beginning of inhalation to loss of consciousness; pain is progressively abolished (dulled); patient remains conscious, can hear and see, feels dream-like; reflexes and respiration normal; amnesia at the end; minor operations (dressing, dental extraction) can be done; best seen with ether and nitrous oxide. Stage II — stage of delirium/excitement: from loss of consciousness to beginning of regular respiration; apparent excitement — patient may shout, struggle, hold breath; irregular respiration, muscle tone increased, jaws tightly closed, breath holding, vomiting, involuntary micturition or defecation, dilated pupils, increased heart rate and BP, laryngospasm; due to inhibition of higher inhibitory centres; most dangerous stage — no stimulus or operative procedure should be done; shortened by rapid IV induction (thiopentone, propofol) and modern agents. Stage III — stage of surgical anaesthesia: from onset of regular respiration to cessation of spontaneous breathing; divided into four planes by character of respiration, eyeball movements, reflexes and pupil size: plane 1 — roving eyeballs, regular deep respiration, loss of eyelash reflex and conjunctival reflex, swallowing absent; plane 2 — eyeballs fixed (centralised), loss of corneal and laryngeal reflexes, respiration regular; plane 3 — pupils begin to dilate, light reflex lost, intercostal muscle paralysis begins, abdominal muscles relaxed; plane 4 — intercostals completely paralysed, purely diaphragmatic shallow respiration, pupils widely dilated; most surgery done in planes 2–3. Stage IV — stage of medullary (respiratory) paralysis: cessation of breathing, failure of circulation, BP falls, pulse feeble, pupils widely dilated and unreactive; death follows unless anaesthetic stopped and respiration/circulation supported. Limitations today: the classic signs are obscured by premedication (opioids, anticholinergics), IV induction agents, neuromuscular blockers, controlled ventilation and newer inhalational agents with rapid onset; depth of anaesthesia is now monitored by clinical signs (BP, HR, lacrimation, sweating, movement), end-tidal anaesthetic concentration (MAC) and processed EEG (bispectral index — BIS 40–60). Recovery passes through the stages in reverse order — risk of excitement, vomiting and laryngospasm during emergence.",
[["Guedel's stages",[["Stage","Extent","Features"],["I Analgesia","Induction → loss of consciousness","Analgesia, conscious, amnesia"],["II Delirium","Loss of consciousness → regular breathing","Excitement, irregular breathing, vomiting, laryngospasm, dilated pupils"],["III Surgical (planes 1–4)","Regular breathing → apnoea","Progressive loss of reflexes, muscle relaxation, intercostal paralysis"],["IV Medullary paralysis","Apnoea → circulatory failure","Respiratory and vasomotor failure"]]],
["Stage III planes",[["Plane","Signs"],["1","Roving eyeballs; eyelash/conjunctival reflexes lost"],["2","Eyeballs fixed; corneal, laryngeal reflexes lost"],["3","Pupils dilating; intercostal paralysis begins"],["4","Complete intercostal paralysis; diaphragmatic breathing"]]]],
{c:"Guedel's stages illustrate the descending CNS depression by anaesthetics; though obscured by modern techniques, they explain the dangers of the excitement stage and of excessive depth, now monitored by MAC and BIS."});

Z("pharm13",1,"ph",["Inhalational anaesthetics are gases or volatile liquids inhaled to produce general anaesthesia; nitrous oxide is a weak analgesic gas, while isoflurane and sevoflurane are potent volatile agents. MAC (minimum alveolar concentration) measures their potency.","MAC is the 'strength meter' — the lower the MAC, the more potent the anaesthetic; blood solubility decides speed — less soluble agents (N2O, desflurane, sevoflurane) work and wear off faster.",["MAC: alveolar concentration at 1 atm preventing movement in 50% of patients to skin incision; potency ∝ 1/MAC ∝ lipid solubility (Meyer–Overton); MAC ↓ with age, hypothermia, opioids, pregnancy; N2O 104%, sevoflurane 2%, isoflurane 1.15%, desflurane 6%, halothane 0.75%.","Blood:gas partition coefficient → speed of induction/recovery: N2O 0.47, desflurane 0.42, sevoflurane 0.65, isoflurane 1.4, halothane 2.3.","N2O: good analgesic, weak anaesthetic (MAC > 100%), used as carrier with O2 (Entonox 50:50 — labour, dental); ADR — diffusion hypoxia (give 100% O2 at end), expansion of air spaces (pneumothorax, middle ear, bowel), megaloblastic anaemia/neuropathy (inactivates B12 — methionine synthase), PONV.","Isoflurane: potent, cheap, stable; vasodilator, ↓ BP, coronary steal (theoretical), pungent (not for induction), muscle relaxation, good for neurosurgery. Sevoflurane: sweet, non-pungent — inhalational induction (children), rapid; compound A (soda lime) nephrotoxicity, fluoride; all volatiles trigger malignant hyperthermia (dantrolene)."],"Sevoflurane = smooth inhalational induction in children; N2O = diffusion hypoxia."],
"Inhalational anaesthetics include gases (nitrous oxide, xenon, cyclopropane — obsolete) and volatile liquids (ether, halothane, enflurane, isoflurane, sevoflurane, desflurane, methoxyflurane). Pharmacokinetics: depth of anaesthesia depends on partial pressure of the agent in the brain, which equilibrates with alveolar partial pressure; factors affecting rate of rise of alveolar concentration and induction — inspired concentration (concentration effect, second gas effect with N2O), alveolar ventilation, blood:gas partition coefficient (solubility in blood — low solubility, e.g., N2O, desflurane, sevoflurane, gives rapid induction and recovery; high solubility, e.g., halothane, ether, slow), cardiac output (high output slows induction), alveolar–venous partial pressure gradient; elimination mainly through lungs (metabolism minor except halothane 20%, methoxyflurane 50%). Minimum alveolar concentration (MAC): the alveolar concentration of an anaesthetic at one atmosphere that prevents movement in response to a standard surgical skin incision in 50% of patients; measure of potency (ED50) — potency inversely proportional to MAC; correlates with oil:gas partition coefficient (Meyer–Overton rule); MACs are additive (e.g., 0.5 MAC N2O + 0.5 MAC isoflurane = 1 MAC); 1.3 MAC prevents movement in 95%; MAC-awake ~0.3–0.4 MAC; MAC-BAR (block adrenergic response) ~1.5 MAC; MAC is decreased by increasing age (6% per decade after 40), hypothermia, hypotension, pregnancy, opioids, benzodiazepines, α2 agonists, acute alcohol intoxication, hyponatraemia, anaemia, lithium; increased by hyperthermia, chronic alcoholism, infants (highest at 6 months), red hair, hypernatraemia, amphetamines, cocaine. Mechanism: enhance inhibitory GABA-A and glycine receptor function, activate two-pore K⁺ channels, inhibit excitatory NMDA (N2O, xenon, ketamine) and neuronal nicotinic receptors; immobility mediated at spinal cord, unconsciousness and amnesia supraspinally. Nitrous oxide (laughing gas): colourless, sweet-smelling, non-irritating, non-inflammable gas (supports combustion) stored in blue cylinders as liquid; MAC 104% — cannot produce surgical anaesthesia alone at normobaric pressure (would need hypoxic mixture); good analgesic (endogenous opioid release, NMDA antagonism) at 50% (Entonox — 50% N2O + 50% O2 — for labour pain, dental procedures, dressings, ambulance analgesia); blood:gas 0.47 — rapid induction and recovery; used as carrier gas in balanced anaesthesia (60–70% with O2) to reduce requirement of potent agents (second gas effect accelerates their uptake); minimal cardiovascular and respiratory depression, no muscle relaxation; not metabolised; adverse effects — diffusion hypoxia (at end of anaesthesia, large volumes of N2O diffuse from blood to alveoli, diluting O2 — prevented by 100% O2 for 5–10 minutes), expansion of closed gas-filled spaces (N2O is 34 times more soluble than nitrogen — enters faster than nitrogen leaves): pneumothorax, bowel obstruction, air embolism, middle ear surgery, pneumoencephalus, intraocular gas bubbles — contraindicated; inactivation of vitamin B12 (oxidises cobalt) inhibiting methionine synthase — megaloblastic anaemia, subacute combined degeneration with prolonged/repeated exposure or abuse, teratogenic concerns; postoperative nausea and vomiting; raised pulmonary vascular resistance; environmental greenhouse gas. Isoflurane: halogenated methyl ethyl ether; non-inflammable, stable, pungent ethereal odour (airway irritation, coughing, breath holding — not suitable for inhalational induction); MAC 1.15%; blood:gas 1.4 (moderately rapid); minimal metabolism (0.2%; no significant hepato- or nephrotoxicity); cardiovascular — dose-dependent fall in BP mainly by peripheral vasodilatation, maintains cardiac output, reflex tachycardia, coronary vasodilator (possible 'coronary steal' — theoretical); does not sensitise heart to adrenaline significantly; respiratory depression, bronchodilator; good muscle relaxation (potentiates NM blockers); cerebral vasodilatation minimal at < 1 MAC with reduced cerebral metabolic rate — favoured in neurosurgery; uterine relaxation; uses — maintenance of anaesthesia (most widely used in India — cheap); adverse effects — hypotension, respiratory depression, malignant hyperthermia trigger, emergence agitation, PONV, rare hepatitis. Sevoflurane: fluorinated methyl isopropyl ether; pleasant, sweet, non-pungent odour; MAC 2%; blood:gas 0.65 — rapid, smooth induction and quick recovery; agent of choice for inhalational induction (children, difficult airway, needle-phobic adults) and day-care anaesthesia; bronchodilator (asthma); maintains heart rate, mild fall in BP, minimal arrhythmogenicity; cerebral autoregulation preserved; metabolised 3–5% by CYP2E1 releasing inorganic fluoride (no clinical nephrotoxicity); reacts with CO2 absorbents (soda lime, baralyme — especially desiccated) to form compound A (vinyl ether, nephrotoxic in rats) — maintain fresh gas flow ≥ 1–2 L/min; adverse effects — emergence delirium in children, PONV, malignant hyperthermia, QT prolongation, seizure-like EEG at high concentrations; more expensive. Other agents: halothane (non-irritant, potent, cheap; sensitises myocardium to catecholamines — arrhythmias, halothane hepatitis, uterine relaxation; largely replaced), desflurane (lowest blood solubility — fastest recovery; pungent, airway irritant, sympathetic stimulation; needs heated vaporiser; high greenhouse impact), ether (inflammable, irritant, slow; obsolete), xenon (ideal but expensive). All potent volatile agents can trigger malignant hyperthermia in susceptible individuals (RYR1 mutation; with succinylcholine) — hypercarbia, rigidity, tachycardia, hyperthermia, acidosis, hyperkalaemia; treatment — stop trigger, hyperventilate with 100% O2, IV dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg repeated, cooling, correct acidosis and hyperkalaemia.",
[["Properties of inhalational agents",[["Agent","MAC (%)","Blood:gas","Key features"],["Nitrous oxide","104","0.47","Analgesic, carrier gas; diffusion hypoxia; B12 inactivation"],["Halothane","0.75","2.3","Arrhythmias with adrenaline, hepatitis"],["Isoflurane","1.15","1.4","Pungent; vasodilator; neurosurgery; cheap"],["Sevoflurane","2.0","0.65","Sweet; inhalational induction; compound A"],["Desflurane","6.0","0.42","Fastest recovery; pungent; sympathetic surge"],["Xenon","71","0.12","Ideal but expensive"]]],
["Factors altering MAC",[["Decrease MAC","Increase MAC"],["Elderly, hypothermia, pregnancy","Infants, hyperthermia"],["Opioids, BZDs, α2 agonists","Chronic alcoholism"],["Acute alcohol, hyponatraemia, anaemia","Hypernatraemia, amphetamines, cocaine"],["Hypotension, lithium","Red hair"]]],
["N2O adverse effects",["Diffusion hypoxia (give 100% O2)","Expansion of closed air spaces (pneumothorax, bowel, middle ear)","B12 inactivation → megaloblastic anaemia, SACD","PONV","Teratogenic concern"]]],
{i:"Inhalational agents remain the mainstay of anaesthetic maintenance; their potency (MAC) and blood solubility determine dosing and speed.",f:"Inspired → alveolar → blood → brain\n Low blood:gas coefficient (N2O, desflurane, sevoflurane) → rapid induction/recovery\n Potency ∝ oil:gas ∝ 1/MAC\n End of case: N2O floods alveoli → diffusion hypoxia → give 100% O2",c:"Nitrous oxide is an analgesic carrier gas with unique risks of diffusion hypoxia and B12 inactivation, while isoflurane and sevoflurane are potent volatile agents for maintenance and induction respectively; MAC guides their dosing."});

Z("pharm13",2,"ph",["Intravenous anaesthetics produce rapid loss of consciousness and are used for induction (and sometimes maintenance) of anaesthesia: propofol (most widely used), thiopentone (ultra-short barbiturate), ketamine (dissociative) and etomidate (cardiostable).","These drugs reach the brain in one arm–brain circulation time (~20 s); their quick wake-up is mainly due to redistribution from brain to muscle and fat, not metabolism.",["Propofol: GABA-A potentiation; rapid smooth induction (1.5–2.5 mg/kg) and recovery, clear-headed, antiemetic; TIVA, day-care, ICU sedation; ADR — pain on injection, hypotension, apnoea, propofol infusion syndrome (acidosis, rhabdomyolysis), lipid emulsion (sepsis).","Thiopentone: ultra-short-acting barbiturate (3–5 mg/kg); redistribution; ↓ ICP and cerebral O2 consumption (neuroprotective), anticonvulsant; ADR — laryngospasm, hypotension, respiratory depression, intra-arterial injection → gangrene (pH 10.5), porphyria (CI), no analgesia (antanalgesic).","Ketamine: NMDA antagonist → dissociative anaesthesia (profound analgesia, amnesia, eyes open, catatonic), sympathetic stimulation (↑ HR, BP), bronchodilator, preserves airway reflexes; uses — shock, asthma, burns dressings, children, field/battlefield, depression (esketamine); ADR — emergence delirium/hallucinations (BZD), ↑ ICP/IOP, salivation.","Etomidate: GABA-A; cardiovascular stability (shock, cardiac disease); ADR — adrenocortical suppression (11β-hydroxylase), myoclonus, PONV, pain on injection."],"Ketamine for shocked/asthmatic; etomidate for cardiac; propofol for day-care; thiopentone for neuro."],
"Intravenous anaesthetics induce anaesthesia rapidly and smoothly (avoiding the excitement stage), are used for induction followed by inhalational maintenance, for total intravenous anaesthesia (TIVA), for short procedures, and for sedation. Classification: (1) inducing agents (fast-acting) — thiopentone sodium, methohexitone (barbiturates), propofol, etomidate; (2) slower-acting agents — benzodiazepines (midazolam, diazepam, lorazepam, remimazolam), dissociative anaesthetic (ketamine), opioid analgesia (fentanyl, remifentanil — neuroleptanalgesia with droperidol); (3) α2 agonist — dexmedetomidine (sedation). Their short duration after a single bolus is due to redistribution: highly lipophilic drug rapidly enters vessel-rich brain, then redistributes to muscle and later fat, so brain levels fall quickly (wake-up in 5–10 min) though elimination t½ is long (cumulation with repeated doses — context-sensitive half-time). Propofol (2,6-diisopropylphenol): oily liquid formulated as 1% emulsion in soybean oil, egg lecithin and glycerol (milky white; supports bacterial growth — strict asepsis; discard within 6–12 h); mechanism — potentiates GABA-A receptor (increases duration of Cl⁻ channel opening), also inhibits NMDA; pharmacokinetics — rapid onset (one arm–brain circulation time, 15–45 s), duration 5–10 min after bolus; rapid hepatic and extrahepatic (lung) metabolism; short context-sensitive half-time — suitable for continuous infusion; dose — induction 1.5–2.5 mg/kg IV, maintenance 4–12 mg/kg/h (TIVA, target-controlled infusion), sedation 25–75 µg/kg/min; actions — smooth induction, rapid clear-headed recovery without hangover (day-care surgery), antiemetic (lower PONV), antipruritic, mild euphoria, reduces ICP and cerebral metabolic rate, anticonvulsant at high doses (though myoclonus can occur), suppresses laryngeal reflexes (facilitates LMA insertion), bronchodilator, no analgesia, no muscle relaxation; uses — induction and maintenance (TIVA) of anaesthesia, day-care and ambulatory surgery, sedation in ICU and for endoscopy/procedures (monitored anaesthesia care), neuroanaesthesia, status epilepticus refractory, patients susceptible to malignant hyperthermia, PONV-prone patients; adverse effects — pain on injection (small veins; reduce with lignocaine, large vein), dose-related hypotension (vasodilatation and myocardial depression — more than thiopentone; caution in hypovolaemia, elderly, cardiac disease), respiratory depression and apnoea, bradycardia, involuntary movements, hyperlipidaemia and pancreatitis with prolonged infusion, bacterial contamination and sepsis, propofol infusion syndrome (high-dose prolonged infusions > 4 mg/kg/h > 48 h, especially children and with catecholamines/steroids — metabolic acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, hyperkalaemia, hyperlipidaemia, cardiac failure, renal failure — often fatal), green urine, abuse potential, allergy (egg/soy — rare); safe in porphyria. Thiopentone sodium: ultra-short-acting thiobarbiturate; yellow powder dissolved in water to 2.5% solution (strongly alkaline pH 10.5; unstable — use within 24 h); mechanism — GABA-A (increases duration), high doses direct activation and inhibition of AMPA; pharmacokinetics — highly lipid soluble, enters brain within 15–20 s (one arm–brain time), consciousness regained in 5–10 min due to redistribution; slowly metabolised in liver (10–15%/h; t½ 10–12 h — hangover, cumulation with repeated doses; zero-order at high doses); dose 3–5 mg/kg IV; actions — rapid pleasant induction, no excitement, poor analgesic (antanalgesic at sub-anaesthetic doses — hyperalgesia), poor muscle relaxant, reduces cerebral blood flow, ICP and cerebral metabolic rate for O2 (cerebral protection), potent anticonvulsant, dose-dependent respiratory depression and apnoea, fall in BP (venodilatation, myocardial depression) with reflex tachycardia, laryngospasm and bronchospasm (parasympathetic dominance; not ideal in asthma); uses — induction of anaesthesia (largely replaced by propofol), short procedures, raised ICP (head injury, neurosurgery — brain protection, barbiturate coma), status epilepticus (refractory), narcoanalysis ('truth serum' — historical); adverse effects — laryngospasm, cough, bronchospasm, hypotension (hypovolaemia, cardiac disease), respiratory depression, shivering, delirium during recovery, prolonged drowsiness, hypersensitivity/anaphylaxis; extravasation causes tissue necrosis; accidental intra-arterial injection — intense vasospasm, crystal formation, thrombosis and gangrene of limb (treat by leaving cannula, injecting saline, papaverine, lignocaine, heparin, sympathetic block); contraindications — acute intermittent porphyria and variegate porphyria (induces ALA synthase — precipitates attack; absolute), status asthmaticus, severe hypovolaemia/shock, severe cardiac disease, airway obstruction. Ketamine: phencyclidine derivative; mechanism — non-competitive NMDA receptor antagonist (blocks glutamate excitation), also opioid, monoaminergic, muscarinic and HCN channel actions; produces dissociative anaesthesia — a cataleptic state with functional dissociation between thalamocortical and limbic systems: profound analgesia (somatic > visceral), amnesia, unconsciousness with eyes open, nystagmus, involuntary limb movements and muscle tone, maintained reflexes (pharyngeal, laryngeal, corneal — but aspiration can occur), spontaneous breathing preserved; pharmacokinetics — IV (1–2 mg/kg; onset 30 s, duration 10–15 min), IM (5–10 mg/kg; onset 3–5 min), oral, intranasal, rectal; metabolised in liver to norketamine (active); actions — cardiovascular stimulation (central sympathetic activation and inhibition of NA reuptake → increased HR, BP and cardiac output; direct myocardial depression may surface in catecholamine-depleted patients), bronchodilatation (sympathomimetic — useful in asthma), increased salivation (give glycopyrrolate), increased cerebral blood flow, ICP and IOP, increased muscle tone; uses — induction in shocked/hypovolaemic or haemodynamically unstable patients (trauma, sepsis), bronchial asthma/status asthmaticus, short procedures and dressing changes in burns (analgesia without airway instrumentation), paediatric sedation and anaesthesia (IM; uncooperative children), field and mass-casualty settings, battlefield anaesthesia (minimal equipment), cardiac tamponade/constrictive pericarditis, analgesia in sub-anaesthetic doses (opioid-sparing, chronic pain, CRPS), procedural sedation, treatment-resistant depression (rapid antidepressant — esketamine nasal spray), refractory status epilepticus; adverse effects — emergence reactions (vivid dreams, hallucinations, delirium, illusions, out-of-body experiences — 10–30% of adults; less in children and elderly; reduced by benzodiazepine premedication, quiet recovery room), hypertension, tachycardia, arrhythmias, increased ICP and IOP, salivation, laryngospasm, nystagmus, muscle rigidity, vomiting, abuse potential (club drug 'Special K'), ulcerative cystitis with chronic abuse; contraindications — hypertension, ischaemic heart disease, aneurysms, raised ICP (relative — modern view less strict), raised IOP/open eye injury, psychosis, eclampsia, hyperthyroidism. Etomidate: carboxylated imidazole; GABA-A modulator; rapid onset, short action; remarkable cardiovascular stability (minimal effect on HR, BP, myocardial contractility) and little respiratory depression — preferred induction agent in cardiac disease, haemodynamically unstable and elderly patients, neurosurgery (reduces ICP); adverse effects — pain on injection, myoclonic movements (common), high incidence of PONV, adrenocortical suppression — reversible inhibition of 11β-hydroxylase (and cholesterol side-chain cleavage), reducing cortisol and aldosterone for 6–24 h after a single dose; prolonged infusions in ICU increased mortality — not used for sedation; caution in sepsis; no analgesia; thrombophlebitis. Midazolam: water-soluble benzodiazepine; IV/IM/intranasal; sedation, anxiolysis and amnesia for procedures, premedication, co-induction, status epilepticus; reversed by flumazenil; respiratory depression. Dexmedetomidine: selective α2 agonist; sedation without respiratory depression ('cooperative sedation') in ICU and procedures; bradycardia, hypotension.",
[["IV anaesthetics compared",[["Feature","Propofol","Thiopentone","Ketamine","Etomidate"],["Mechanism","GABA-A","GABA-A","NMDA antagonist","GABA-A"],["Induction dose","1.5–2.5 mg/kg","3–5 mg/kg","1–2 mg/kg IV","0.2–0.3 mg/kg"],["Analgesia","No","No (antanalgesic)","Profound","No"],["BP / HR","↓↓ BP","↓ BP, ↑ HR","↑ BP, ↑ HR","Stable"],["Respiration","Apnoea","Depression","Preserved","Minimal depression"],["ICP","↓","↓↓","↑","↓"],["Airway","Suppresses reflexes","Laryngospasm","Reflexes maintained, bronchodilator","—"],["Special","Antiemetic, TIVA, day-care","Brain protection, anticonvulsant","Dissociation, emergence delirium","Adrenal suppression, myoclonus"],["CI / caution","Hypovolaemia, egg/soy allergy?","Porphyria, asthma, shock","Raised ICP/IOP, IHD, psychosis","Sepsis (adrenal)"]]],
["Choice of induction agent",[["Situation","Agent"],["Routine / day-care","Propofol"],["Shock, hypovolaemia, trauma","Ketamine (or etomidate)"],["Asthma","Ketamine, propofol"],["Cardiac disease, elderly unstable","Etomidate"],["Raised ICP, neuroprotection","Thiopentone, propofol"],["Children, uncooperative","Ketamine IM or sevoflurane"],["Porphyria","Propofol, ketamine (avoid thiopentone, etomidate?)"],["Burns dressing","Ketamine"]]]],
{i:"IV induction agents are chosen by patient physiology; their brief action after a bolus results from redistribution.",x:[["Intra-arterial thiopentone",["Pain, blanching, gangrene risk","Leave cannula; inject saline, papaverine/lignocaine","Heparin, sympathetic block (stellate/brachial plexus)"]]],f:"IV bolus → brain (vessel-rich, ~20 s) → unconsciousness\n → redistribution to muscle → fat → awakening (5–10 min)\n → hepatic metabolism (elimination) — cumulation with repeated doses (thiopentone)",c:"Propofol is the standard induction agent for smooth, clear-headed recovery; thiopentone offers cerebral protection; ketamine provides analgesia with cardiovascular stimulation for shocked or asthmatic patients; and etomidate offers cardiovascular stability at the cost of adrenal suppression."});

Z("pharm13",3,"ph",["Pre-anaesthetic medication is the use of drugs before anaesthesia to relieve anxiety, reduce secretions, provide analgesia, prevent vomiting and aspiration, and make induction smoother and safer.","It's preparing the patient: calm the mind (benzodiazepines), dry the airway (glycopyrrolate), ease pain (opioids), protect the stomach (antacids, H2/PPI) and prevent vomiting (antiemetics).",["Anxiolytics/sedatives: midazolam, diazepam, lorazepam (amnesia), promethazine; α2 agonists — clonidine, dexmedetomidine.","Opioids: morphine, fentanyl, pethidine — analgesia, reduce anaesthetic requirement; ADR — respiratory depression, vomiting.","Anticholinergics: glycopyrrolate (preferred — no CNS), atropine, hyoscine — ↓ secretions, prevent vagal bradycardia/laryngospasm.","Aspiration prophylaxis: H2 blockers (ranitidine/famotidine), PPIs, metoclopramide, sodium citrate (non-particulate antacid) — pregnancy, obesity, emergency; antiemetics — ondansetron, dexamethasone; others — steroids, antibiotics (prophylaxis), β-blockers."],"Premedication aims '5 A's': Anxiolysis, Amnesia, Analgesia, Antisialagogue, Antiemetic (+ anti-aspiration)."],
"Pre-anaesthetic medication (premedication) refers to drugs given before the administration of anaesthesia (usually 30–60 minutes before or on the evening before surgery) to make anaesthesia safer, smoother and more pleasant. Aims: (1) relief of anxiety and apprehension (anxiolysis) and sedation; (2) amnesia for perioperative events; (3) analgesia (pre-emptive) and reduced anaesthetic requirement; (4) reduction of salivary and respiratory secretions (antisialagogue — important with irritant agents like ether, ketamine, fibreoptic intubation); (5) prevention of vagal reflexes — bradycardia, laryngospasm (induced by succinylcholine, traction on eye muscles, peritoneum, intubation); (6) prevention of postoperative nausea and vomiting; (7) reduction of gastric volume and acidity to prevent aspiration pneumonitis (Mendelson syndrome); (8) facilitation of smooth induction and recovery; (9) prophylaxis of allergic reactions, infection (antibiotics), thromboembolism; (10) continuation of essential medications (antihypertensives, β-blockers, steroid cover). Drugs: (A) sedative–anxiolytics — benzodiazepines: diazepam 5–10 mg oral (night before/morning), lorazepam 1–2 mg (marked amnesia), midazolam 0.05–0.1 mg/kg IM/IV or 0.5 mg/kg oral (children) — most commonly used: rapid, short-acting, anterograde amnesia, minimal cardiorespiratory depression; antihistamines: promethazine 25–50 mg IM (sedative, antiemetic, anticholinergic), hydroxyzine; α2 agonists: clonidine (oral 2–4 µg/kg; sedation, anxiolysis, haemodynamic stability, reduces anaesthetic and analgesic requirement), dexmedetomidine; barbiturates (historical); (B) opioid analgesics — morphine 10 mg IM, pethidine 50–100 mg IM, fentanyl 1–2 µg/kg IV, butorphanol, pentazocine: provide analgesia in painful conditions, sedation, smooth induction, reduce anaesthetic dose; disadvantages — respiratory depression, nausea and vomiting, constipation, urinary retention, postural hypotension, delayed recovery, biliary spasm; (C) anticholinergics (antisialagogues and vagolytics) — glycopyrrolate 0.2–0.4 mg IM/IV (quaternary — does not cross BBB, no CNS effects, potent antisialagogue, less tachycardia; preferred), atropine 0.6 mg IM/IV (crosses BBB — CNS excitement in elderly, tachycardia, hyperthermia in children; antivagal), hyoscine 0.4 mg (sedation, amnesia, antiemetic, antisialagogue; confusion in elderly); routine use is now selective (paediatric, ENT/dental, ketamine, eye surgery — oculocardiac reflex); (D) drugs to prevent aspiration — H2 blockers (ranitidine 150 mg oral night before and morning/famotidine) and PPIs (pantoprazole 40 mg) to raise gastric pH > 2.5 and reduce volume; prokinetics — metoclopramide 10 mg (enhances gastric emptying, LES tone); non-particulate antacid — 0.3 M sodium citrate 30 mL just before induction (caesarean section); indicated in pregnancy, obesity, GERD, hiatus hernia, diabetes (gastroparesis), emergency surgery (full stomach), along with rapid sequence induction with cricoid pressure; (E) antiemetics — ondansetron 4 mg IV, dexamethasone 4–8 mg IV at induction, droperidol, metoclopramide, aprepitant for high-risk PONV (Apfel score: female, non-smoker, history of PONV/motion sickness, postoperative opioids); (F) others — corticosteroids (steroid cover for patients on long-term steroids; hydrocortisone 100 mg), antibiotics (surgical prophylaxis — cefazolin 1–2 g within 60 min before incision), antihistamines + steroids for atopic/history of allergy, β-blockers and statins (continue in cardiac patients), anticoagulant management, EMLA cream for IV cannulation in children, gabapentinoids/paracetamol (multimodal pre-emptive analgesia). Fasting guidelines: clear fluids up to 2 h, breast milk 4 h, light meal/formula 6 h, fatty meal 8 h before anaesthesia.",
[["Drugs for premedication",[["Purpose","Drugs"],["Anxiolysis, amnesia","Midazolam, diazepam, lorazepam"],["Sedation","Promethazine, clonidine, dexmedetomidine"],["Analgesia","Morphine, pethidine, fentanyl"],["Antisialagogue, antivagal","Glycopyrrolate, atropine, hyoscine"],["Aspiration prophylaxis","Ranitidine/famotidine, PPI, metoclopramide, sodium citrate"],["Antiemetic","Ondansetron, dexamethasone, droperidol"],["Others","Steroid cover, antibiotics, continue β-blockers"]]],
["Glycopyrrolate vs atropine",[["Feature","Glycopyrrolate","Atropine"],["Structure","Quaternary","Tertiary"],["Crosses BBB","No","Yes"],["Antisialagogue","More potent","Less"],["Tachycardia","Less","More"],["CNS effects","None","Excitement, delirium (elderly)"]]]],
{c:"Pre-anaesthetic medication is tailored to each patient to relieve anxiety, provide analgesia, dry secretions, block vagal reflexes and prevent aspiration and vomiting, making anaesthesia safer and smoother."});

Z("pharm13",4,"ph",["Balanced anaesthesia combines several drugs, each to achieve one component of anaesthesia (unconsciousness, analgesia, muscle relaxation, reflex suppression) at lower doses with fewer side effects; dissociative anaesthesia, produced by ketamine, is a state of profound analgesia and amnesia with the patient appearing awake but disconnected.","Instead of one drug in a big, toxic dose, balanced anaesthesia uses a 'team' — an induction agent, an opioid, a muscle relaxant and an inhaled agent — each doing its job in small amounts.",["Components ('triad'/'quadrad'): hypnosis/unconsciousness, analgesia, muscle relaxation, autonomic reflex suppression (+ amnesia).","Typical: premedication (midazolam, glycopyrrolate) → induction (propofol/thiopentone) → opioid (fentanyl) → muscle relaxant (succinylcholine for intubation, then vecuronium/atracurium/rocuronium) → maintenance (N2O + O2 + isoflurane/sevoflurane) → reversal (neostigmine + glycopyrrolate or sugammadex).","Advantages: rapid smooth induction and recovery, less cardiorespiratory depression, better muscle relaxation without deep anaesthesia, less PONV, tailored.","Dissociative anaesthesia (ketamine): functional dissociation of thalamocortical and limbic systems — profound analgesia, amnesia, eyes open, catalepsy, reflexes and breathing preserved, ↑ BP/HR; emergence hallucinations; neuroleptanalgesia = droperidol + fentanyl (+ N2O = neuroleptanaesthesia)."],"Balanced = 'Hypnosis + Analgesia + Relaxation'; dissociative = ketamine."],
"Balanced anaesthesia: no single agent provides all the desirable components of general anaesthesia — unconsciousness (hypnosis), amnesia, analgesia, skeletal muscle relaxation and suppression of autonomic and somatic reflexes — without producing dangerous cardiorespiratory depression at the high doses required (e.g., deep ether or halothane anaesthesia). The concept (Lundy, 1926) is to combine several drugs, each in smaller doses, to achieve specific components, thereby maximising benefits and minimising adverse effects. Components and drugs: (1) premedication — anxiolytic (midazolam), antisialagogue (glycopyrrolate), antiemetic, aspiration prophylaxis; (2) induction — rapid IV agent (propofol, thiopentone, etomidate, ketamine) or inhalational (sevoflurane) for unconsciousness; (3) analgesia — opioids (fentanyl, morphine, remifentanil), nitrous oxide, adjuncts (paracetamol, NSAIDs, ketamine low dose, dexmedetomidine, lignocaine infusion), regional blocks (multimodal analgesia); (4) muscle relaxation — succinylcholine (rapid intubation) or rocuronium; non-depolarising blockers (vecuronium, atracurium, cisatracurium, rocuronium) for maintenance — allow light planes of anaesthesia with controlled ventilation; (5) maintenance — inhalational agents (isoflurane, sevoflurane, desflurane in O2 + N2O or air) or TIVA (propofol + remifentanil infusions); (6) reflex control — opioids, β-blockers (esmolol), lignocaine to blunt intubation response; (7) reversal and recovery — anticholinesterase (neostigmine) with antimuscarinic (glycopyrrolate) or sugammadex (rocuronium/vecuronium encapsulation), naloxone if opioid depression, antiemetics; postoperative analgesia. Advantages: rapid and pleasant induction, avoidance of excitement stage, adequate analgesia and muscle relaxation at light anaesthesia, less cardiovascular and respiratory depression, faster recovery, reduced PONV and organ toxicity, flexibility for patient condition, use of controlled ventilation; disadvantages — multiple drugs, interactions, risk of awareness under anaesthesia (with muscle relaxants), need for skilled monitoring and ventilatory support, residual neuromuscular blockade. Dissociative anaesthesia: a state produced by ketamine (and phencyclidine), characterised by functional and electrophysiological dissociation between the thalamocortical (sensory-cognitive) and limbic systems via NMDA receptor blockade; features — profound somatic analgesia, amnesia, sedation and unresponsiveness while the patient may appear awake (eyes open, nystagmus, staring), catalepsy, increased muscle tone and purposeless movements, preserved protective airway reflexes and spontaneous respiration (though aspiration can occur), sympathetic stimulation (tachycardia, hypertension, increased cardiac output), bronchodilatation, increased salivation, ICP and IOP; emergence phenomena — vivid dreams, hallucinations, delirium (reduced by benzodiazepines, quiet surroundings); uses — induction in shock, asthma, children, burns dressings, field and disaster settings, short painful procedures, low resource settings (no need for elaborate equipment). Neuroleptanalgesia: a state of profound analgesia, tranquillity and indifference with the patient conscious and able to obey commands, produced by combining a neuroleptic (droperidol) with a potent opioid (fentanyl) — Innovar; used for minor procedures, endoscopy, burns dressings and awake intubation; adding nitrous oxide produces neuroleptanaesthesia (unconsciousness); disadvantages — respiratory depression, chest wall rigidity (fentanyl), extrapyramidal reactions and QT prolongation (droperidol), hypotension; largely replaced by modern sedation techniques (midazolam/propofol + fentanyl, dexmedetomidine). Monitored anaesthesia care and conscious sedation are related modern concepts.",
[["Balanced anaesthesia",[["Component","Drugs"],["Premedication","Midazolam, glycopyrrolate, ondansetron, H2 blocker"],["Induction (hypnosis)","Propofol, thiopentone, etomidate, ketamine, sevoflurane"],["Analgesia","Fentanyl, morphine, remifentanil, N2O, paracetamol, NSAIDs"],["Muscle relaxation","Succinylcholine, rocuronium, vecuronium, atracurium"],["Maintenance","N2O/O2 + isoflurane/sevoflurane or TIVA (propofol)"],["Reversal","Neostigmine + glycopyrrolate; sugammadex; naloxone"]]],
["Dissociative vs neuroleptanalgesia",[["Feature","Dissociative (ketamine)","Neuroleptanalgesia (droperidol + fentanyl)"],["Consciousness","Dissociated, eyes open","Conscious, calm, cooperative"],["Analgesia","Profound","Profound"],["Respiration","Preserved","Depressed"],["Cardiovascular","↑ BP, HR","↓ BP"],["Problem","Emergence hallucinations","Chest rigidity, EPS, QT"]]]],
{c:"Balanced anaesthesia uses multiple drugs to achieve hypnosis, analgesia, relaxation and reflex control at lower individual doses, while ketamine's dissociative anaesthesia offers analgesia with preserved airway and circulation for special situations."});
